 \begin{abstract}
-Mountain trip planning requires the integration of route characteristics, weather forecasts, trail restrictions, and team capabilities. These inputs differ in spatial coverage, validity periods, and reliability, making unsupported or outdated recommendations particularly problematic. This paper proposes TrailGuard TW, an evidence-grounded decision-support architecture for mountain trip planning in Taiwan. The architecture separates language-model-based requirement elicitation and explanation from deterministic computation and constraint enforcement. Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools provide typed interfaces to environmental data, route information, retrieval services, and analytical functions. A temporal evidence representation associates each claim with its source, applicable interval, and transformation history. A rule-based decision gate rejects candidates that violate authoritative restrictions and withholds recommendations when essential evidence is unresolved. Feasible alternatives are packaged with segment-level explanations, retreat checkpoints, and an auditable evidence ledger. We specify the eight functional modules, tool contracts, decision logic, and a planned evaluation comprising 90 route--team combinations and 30 adversarial cases. The contribution is a system design and evaluation protocol; no implemented deployment, measured performance, or field safety validation is claimed. The framework provides a testable basis for studying traceability, constraint compliance, and the limits of language-model-assisted outdoor planning.
+Mountain trip planning combines route characteristics, forecasts, access restrictions, and team capabilities whose spatial and temporal scopes may not agree. A fluent recommendation can therefore be unsupported even when it cites relevant documents. This paper proposes TrailGuard TW, an evidence-grounded decision-support architecture for mountain trips in Taiwan. A language model elicits requirements and coordinates typed Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools, while deterministic functions compute derived values and enforce restrictions. Temporal evidence records link observations to route segments, effective intervals, and computation histories. Before candidate ranking, a three-state gate distinguishes prohibited itineraries (NO-GO), unresolved essential evidence (HOLD), and candidates passing the configured checks (ADMISSIBLE). The design specifies eight modules, tool contracts, a bounded planning algorithm, and an auditable output package. A structured comparison relates the architecture to retrieval-augmented generation and tool-using agents. A planned evaluation combines 90 route--team scenarios and 30 adversarial cases, with controlled baselines and explicit metric denominators. Synthetic traces illustrate intended behavior; they are not experimental results. This work contributes a reproducible system specification and evaluation protocol, while leaving implementation, field validation, and measured effectiveness to future study.
 \end{abstract}
